====9th====
*[[Matt Murdock]] wakes up in [[Matt Murdock's Apartment|his apartment]] to the sound of [[Foggy Nelson]] calling him. Over the phone, Nelson demands Murdock get up and be ready to see their potential [[Nelson and Murdock Law Office|new office]].{{Ref|DD101}}<ref name="DDS1 Nov-Dec 2014 Placement">There is conflicting evidence as to whether ''[[Daredevil (TV series)|Daredevil]]'': [[Daredevil (TV series)/Season One|Season One]] takes place in either late 2014 or early 2015.
*In ''[[Into the Ring]]'', Karen Page states, "The last two years have transformed [Union Allied]," with Matt Murdock replying, "Yeah, the world watched half of New York get destroyed," suggesting that two years have passed since the Battle of New York on May 4, 2012 (see [[2012]] references). Although it would still be less than three full years since the Battle of New York if this were said in early 2015, this line would strongly suggest the season occurs during the calendar year of 2014.
*In ''[[The Man in the Box]]'', Wilson Fisk says to Murdock, "We met once before. [...] It was at an art gallery, several months ago," referring to the events of ''[[Speak of the Devil]]''. Additionally, statements made by [https://tvline.com/interviews/daredevil-season-2-charlie-cox-preview-punisher-elektra-694334/ Charlie Cox] and [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xoOZWkhqTr8&t=251s Deborah Ann Woll] would suggest that roughly "six months" pass between Season One and Season Two. With the main events of Season Two firmly dated to October-November 2015 (see [[2015]] references), this would suggest Season One occurs early in the same year.
*Over the course of the season, the general weather and climate goes from temperate to cold, with clothing worn by characters becoming heavier as the season goes along and barren trees being shown in the final couple episodes. This would imply that the season occurs closer to the end of the year when it begins to get colder (late 2014), rather than the beginning of the year when it begins to get warmer (early 2015).
*In ''[[Reunion (Daredevil)|Reunion]]'', in October 2017 (see [[2017]] references), Benjamin Donovan states, "For the past two years, ever since his unjust conviction on racketeering charges, [Fisk] has maintained his innocence and fought to clear his good name." This would seemingly imply it's been about two years since Fisk was arrested at the end of ''[[Daredevil (episode)|Daredevil]]'', so around 2015, with this being the moment Fisk would've began maintaining his innocence. However, with Donovan technically only referring to the amount of time since Fisk's conviction, which happened a while after Fisk's arrest (see 2015 references), this does allow some leeway for the possibility of Fisk being arrested in 2014, with his conviction happening in 2015.
*While speaking to Felix Manning in ''[[One Last Shot]]'', Fisk states, "You protected her... two years. You kept Vanessa safe when I couldn't." With Fisk being arrested and thus losing the ability to protect Marianna at the end of ''Daredevil'', this would again suggest it's been about two years since Fisk was arrested, so 2015; however, with Fisk specifically referring to the amount of time Manning himself has protected Marianna and it never being explicitly stated when exactly his protection of her began, this, as well, allows some leeway for the possibility of Fisk being arrested in 2014, with Manning perhaps beginning his protection of Marianna around early 2015.
*On January 23, 2015, the WHiH Newsfront Google+ account posted, "''[[:File:Whihgoogle04.png|Crime rate finally stable in New York. Are the Avengers to thank? Find out tonight at 10.]]''" Charlie Cox [https://tvline.com/interviews/daredevil-season-2-charlie-cox-preview-punisher-elektra-694334/ stated] that the crime rate in New York City plummeted as a result of criminals being aware of Daredevil's work following the end of Season One. Based on WHiH Newsfront posting about holidays on their real-world dates and posting about Scott Lang's July 17, 2015 release from prison in the lead up to said date in real life, WHiH Newsfront can be taken to have posted in real time with their other posts as well, meaning this post referencing Daredevil was also posted on January 23, 2015 in-universe. Coupled with Cox's statement, as well as the fact that it would make very little sense for crime to stabilize in the middle of the season if it were happening in early 2015, this post would support the idea that Season One is happening in late 2014, with criminal activity across New York plummeting after Fisk's imprisonment and Daredevil becoming a known figure amongst criminals across the city in the time leading up to this post.
*Stick abandoned Murdock when he was a child around 1995 (see [[1990s]] references). In ''[[Stick (episode)|Stick]]'', it's stated twice that this happened "20 years" ago, suggesting it is now around 2015, though this figure could be a result of rounding.
*''New York Bulletin'' newspaper props for Season One, [https://kissthemgoodbye.net/marveltvshows/albums/Daredevil/Season%201/Daredevil%20Episode%2013%20Daredevil/D_S1_E13_0962.jpg one of which] is briefly shown on-screen, contain adverts for a "[https://marvel.propstoreauction.com/lot-details/index/catalog/198/lot/42461 Fall] [https://marvel.propstoreauction.com/lot-details/index/catalog/198/lot/42489 Spe][https://marvel.propstoreauction.com/lot-details/index/catalog/198/lot/42537 cial]" on dental implants, suggesting that the season occurs during the fall. Additionally, throughout some of these props, there are mentions of sporting events such as the US Open, the MLB postseason, and NFL finals, all of which happen at some point during the fall, and there is a date of "Tuesday, October 14, 2014" next to a story about Jay-Z and Beyonce visiting the Louvre, which happened in real life around said date. Although not all of these events happen around the exact same time in the fall, meaning they cannot all work in conjunction with one another, they would all strongly suggest a timeframe of fall 2014.
*In ''Speak of the Devil'', Stewart Schmidt and Joseph Pike's IDs are shown in small dates to have been issued on "01/01/2015" and will expire on "12/31/2015", suggesting it should be after January 1, 2015.
*In ''[[The Ones We Leave Behind]]'', as Murdock chases after the car, [https://kissthemgoodbye.net/marveltvshows/albums/Daredevil/Season%201/Daredevil%20Episode%2012%20The%20Ones%20We%20Leave%20Behind/D_S1_E12_0498.jpg barren trees with decorative lights] can be seen in the background. Additionally, in ''Daredevil'', outside the Nelson & Murdock office, [https://kissthemgoodbye.net/marveltvshows/albums/Daredevil/Season%201/Daredevil%20Episode%2013%20Daredevil/D_S1_E13_0962.jpg a window can be seen with Christmas decorations] (red and green ribbons, yellow decorative lights) and [https://kissthemgoodbye.net/marveltvshows/albums/Daredevil/Season%201/Daredevil%20Episode%2013%20Daredevil/D_S1_E13_0980.jpg a large pine tree], potentially covered in decorative lights, can be seen down the street. During the scene, Page is holding a newspaper with a mention of an NFL "SPORTS FINAL," suggesting it's toward the end of the NFL football season, which ends around late December. All of this would heavily indicate these two episodes are happening around a December timeframe, with decorative lights and Christmas lights being put up for the holidays and the NFL season nearing its end. This would only work with late 2014.
*In ''[[Shadows in the Glass]]'', an article for the ''New York Bulletin'' is shown to have been published on "[[:File:NYB-SitG.png|September 5, 2015]]." Though the date is incorrect whatever the case, this would maybe slightly favor early 2015 over late 2014, with at least the 2015 part being correct.
*Murdock and Nelson left Landman and Zack shortly before acquiring their law licenses on July 20, 2014 (see other references). With the season occurring early in their legal careers, this would suggest it hasn't been very long since they left Landman and Zack, which would fit better with late 2014 as opposed to early 2015.
*A promotional advert for Season One shows that Union Allied has plans for "[[:File:Union Allied Promotional 2.png|Spring 2015]]." Though this would work just about as well with both late 2014 and early 2015, the latter would maybe work a little bit better, with the events of the show happening closer to its real world Spring 2015 release date. 
*In ''Daredevil'', following Fisk's arrest, Nelson states, "Fisk still needs to go to trial. Just getting a court date on a case this big could take a year." Although Fisk does say in ''The Man in the Box'' that Season One was "several months ago," meaning Nelson's estimate has to have been incorrect whatever the case, this line would still suggest that the time between ''Daredevil'' and Fisk's trial/conviction, which happened no later than around mid-late 2015 (see 2015 references), is perhaps slightly longer than just a few short months if it were early 2015, thus slightly favoring late 2014.
Ultimately, the best overall option, when weighing the evidence together and looking for any possible reconciliations or leeway that may exist, is reasoned out to be late 2014 over early 2015.<br>
As mentioned above, Cox and Woll both stated that approximately six months pass between seasons, with Fisk also stating in ''The Man in the Box'' that it has been "several months" since he first met Murdock in ''Speak of the Devil''. With this in mind, it can be taken that Season One occurs as late in 2014 as is realistically possible in order to best fit these statements. While Cox and Woll's "six months" must therefore be taken as incorrect whatever the case, for Fisk's statement to somewhat work, it can be taken that he and Murdock first met in December 2014, 11 months prior to November 2015, not yet a full year. Not only does this work well with the cold weather toward the tail end of the season, but it also works well with the holidays decorations in the final two episodes. With the season spanning about a month, this would place the season as occurring from November-December 2014.<br>
The main events of ''Into the Ring'' begin with Murdock and Nelson acquiring their office on the same day as Page's arrest. The next day, after Murdock asks why Page has yet to be charged, Nelson states that "it's the weekend." However, Tracy Farnum tells her father earlier that day that she will swing by his apartment "this weekend," which she does at the end of the episode on the same day a weekday edition of the ''New York Bulletin'' is published. In ''[[Condemned]]'', it's shown on a newspaper that the kidnapping of the boy at the end of ''Into the Ring'' occurred on a Friday, which fits both as being a weekday and a part of the weekend. Working backward, this means that Farnum's line can occur no earlier than the preceding Monday, which would go against Nelson's line. It can be taken that Nelson is instead referring to Page's arrest as having occurred over the weekend, on a Sunday, and that it is currently Monday. This would place the main events of ''Into the Ring'' on a Sunday-Tuesday, with a time jump of a few days to the following Friday. During the Tuesday portion of the episode, the Empire State Building can be seen to be lit red, white, and blue. In November 2014, the Empire State Building was lit this way on the night of Tuesday, November 11 for Veteran's Day, which fits nicely, so it can be taken that ''Into the Ring'' spans November 9-14, 2014.<br>
From here, ''[[Cut Man]]'' is said to pick up two days later, so Sunday, November 16, 2014, and ''[[Rabbit in a Snowstorm (episode)|Rabbit in a Snowstorm]]'' begins the following morning, so Monday, November 17, 2014. A small time jump then occurs to the start of John Healy's trial, whose cuts and bruises are still present during which, suggesting this is no more than a week later. With courthouses typically only open on weekdays and Healy's trial lasting two consecutive days, his trial ends up working best as being the following Thursday-Friday, so November 20-21, 2014. ''[[In the Blood]]'' to ''Condemned'' occurs over the following Sunday-Wednesday, based on a newspaper clipping in the latter episode saying that the shootout at Veles Taxi occurred at 3:35 AM on a Tuesday, so November 23-26, 2014.<br>
The main events of ''Stick'' pick up shortly afterward on a weekday, followed by ''Shadows in the Glass'' the next day, which features a small undefined gap partway through the episode. After this gap, eight consecutive days occur through to the end of ''The Ones We Leave Behind'', which ends on a weekday based on Mitchell Ellison's son being in school that day. Taking advantage of the small gap in ''Shadows in the Glass'', ''Stick'' can be taken to be Friday, November 28, 2014, with ''Shadows in the Glass'' beginning the next day, Saturday, November 29, 2014 before jumping to Monday, December 1, 2014, resulting in ''The Ones We Leave Behind'' finishing on Monday, December 8, 2014. This ends up placing Murdock and Fisk's meeting at the Scene Contempo Gallery on Wednesday, December 3, 2014, just about fitting the ideal December 2014 placement for this scene.<br>
''Daredevil'' begins with Ben Urich's funeral, and the following day, Brett Mahoney states that the end of ''The Ones We Leave Behind'' was "a few days ago," making this no later than Sunday, December 14, 2014. The next day, many members of the ''New York Bulletin'' can be seen at work, implying it is a weekday, so it can be taken that this is now Monday, December 15, 2014. The episode concludes the next day, so December 16, 2014. This just about fits with still being in fall, with the ''New York Bulletin'' article published that day still featuring ads for the dental "Fall Special."</ref>
